= Complex system =

== Overview ==
A '''complex system''' is a system whose collective behavior depends strongly on interactions among components and often exhibits nonlinear, multiscale, adaptive, or emergent dynamics that cannot be understood adequately by treating components independently.

There is no single universally accepted definition across all disciplines.

== Interactions matter ==
A system can have many components without being complex in the scientific sense.

Complexity often arises because interactions create:

* feedback loops;
* nonlinear effects;
* path dependence;
* collective behavior;
* heterogeneous responses;
* changing network structure;
* multiple scales;
* adaptation.

The pattern of interaction can matter more than raw component count.

== Complex versus complicated ==
A complicated engineered object can contain many parts yet remain largely decomposable.

A complex system may contain simpler parts whose interactions generate collective behavior that resists straightforward decomposition.

Thus:

<pre>
complex ≠ merely complicated
</pre>

== Common characteristics ==
Frequently studied characteristics include:

* [[Emergence]];
* [[Self-organization]];
* [[Feedback]];
* [[Network|network structure]];
* nonlinearity;
* heterogeneity;
* adaptation;
* robustness and fragility.

These are common features, not a checklist that every complex system must satisfy.

== Examples ==
Examples include ecosystems, brains, economies, social systems, power grids, transportation systems, biological regulatory systems, and some technological networks.

The appropriate system boundary depends on the question being studied.

== Related concepts ==
[[System boundary]], [[Network]], [[Feedback]], [[Emergence]], [[Self-organization]], [[Robustness]], and [[Adaptation (systems)]] define the systems/complexity neighborhood.

